  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 6♣. West's wins trick 1 and continues with the ♠A for South to ruff. South's only loser is in hearts. He will need either a 3-3 diamond break or some kind of squeeze to succeed.\n\nSouth should draw trump and probe the diamond distribution by cashing 2 of dummy's top diamonds. When West turns out to have diamonds stopped, a squeeze becomes necessary. If West has both heart honors he will be the victim of a simple squeeze between hearts and diamonds.\n\nWest cannot have both heart honors, however. West is a passed hand and has already shown up with the ♠A, ♠K, and a singleton club. With both heart honors as well he would have opened the bidding.\n\nFortunately, there is an alternative. If West was dealt 1 heart honor, a double guard squeeze will operate. South should run his trumps, taking care to discard the ♥J and to keep dummy's remaining spade.\n\nOn the last trump, West has no good answer. A diamond discard is immediately fatal. A discard of the ♥K will create a finessing position against East's ♥Q.\n\nIf West discards a spade East becomes the only defender with a spade stop. South discards dummy's small diamond. When South cashes dummy's last diamond winner, East is squeezed in the majors.\n\nThe double guard squeeze matures with 1 loser. A victim is squeezed in 3 suits. 1 of these suits is stopped by both defenders, but by discarding from that suit, the victim exposes his partner to a simple squeeze. In a second threat suit, the squeeze victim is protecting a finessing position against his partner. In the third threat suit the victim is the only defender with a stopper.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠K against 6♣. West's wins trick 1 and continues with the ♠A for South to ruff. South's only loser is in hearts. He will need either a 3-3 diamond break or some kind of squeeze to succeed.",
    "South should draw trump and probe the diamond distribution by cashing 2 of dummy's top diamonds. When West turns out to have diamonds stopped, a squeeze becomes necessary. If West has both heart honors he will be the victim of a simple squeeze between hearts and diamonds.",
    "West cannot have both heart honors, however. West is a passed hand and has already shown up with the ♠A, ♠K, and a singleton club. With both heart honors as well he would have opened the bidding.",
    "Fortunately, there is an alternative. If West was dealt 1 heart honor, a double guard squeeze will operate. South should run his trumps, taking care to discard the ♥J and to keep dummy's remaining spade.",
    "On the last trump, West has no good answer. A diamond discard is immediately fatal. A discard of the ♥K will create a finessing position against East's ♥Q.",
    "If West discards a spade East becomes the only defender with a spade stop. South discards dummy's small diamond. When South cashes dummy's last diamond winner, East is squeezed in the majors.",
    "The double guard squeeze matures with 1 loser. A victim is squeezed in 3 suits. 1 of these suits is stopped by both defenders, but by discarding from that suit, the victim exposes his partner to a simple squeeze. In a second threat suit, the squeeze victim is protecting a finessing position against his partner. In the third threat suit the victim is the only defender with a stopper."
